      ⍝ regression (Bugs28 #100(s)): (⊂NAME)←C, the standard idiom for
      ⍝ nesting a whole array C into a single (possibly indexed) target
      ⍝ as one atomic item, gave a bare SYNTAX ERROR whenever C was not
      ⍝ itself a scalar. Source: Bif_F12_PARTITION::do_eval_B() treats
      ⍝ any scalar B whose one cell is an LvalCell as "not simple" (an
      ⍝ LvalCell genuinely isn't a plain data cell) and took the
      ⍝ "nested: clone and copy" branch -- but that branch clones the
      ⍝ pointed-to VALUE, discarding the live reference, so the ⊂
      ⍝ result's own cell became a plain PointerCell instead of an
      ⍝ LvalCell, and the later selective-assignment code (which
      ⍝ requires the target cell to itself be an lvalue) had nothing to
      ⍝ write through. Enclosing a scalar is a no-op regardless of
      ⍝ whether it happens to be a live lvalue reference, so the cell
      ⍝ is now copied directly (preserving its lvalue-ness) instead.
      ⍝
      B←5
      (⊂B)←1 2 3
      B
 1 2 3 

      V←1 2 3 4
      (⊂V[2])←10 20 30
      V
 1  10 20 30  3 4 

      )ERASE B V

